    <GaugingStation location="Cropple How" name="Esk" number="74007">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Velocity area station gauging steep, rural flashy catchment in the western Lake District.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">An open channel site where a rough stone ford downstream forms some control for low flows. Changes to the ford and problems with gauging sites have necessitated frequent rating changes at the site. Due to the nature of the rough control, there is low confidence in the low flow data, although no known major problems. Low Flows - Cobble accretion problems at control variably affecting levels and low flow derivation. High Flows - no known problems up to bank full. Above bank full, rating does not include bypass flow. Multiple peak flow ratings across period of record.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Velocity area station, opened October 1974. Channel 17.3m across, banks about 2m.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2020 (WINFAP Files v9).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Flashy regime with no known artificial influences.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">A steep rural, flashy, catchment with geology comprising impervious Ordovician andesitic lavas and tuffs with massive granitic intrusion, virtually drift free. Mountainous catchment (maximum altitude at Scafell Pike), with no industry or urbanisation, supports rough pasture and moorland for sheep grazing; grassland in valley. Catchment includes Burnmoor Tarn. No catchment changes known.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">N</Comment>
        </Comments>
        <Usages>
            <Usage notes="Gauged close to QMED. Good fit to gaugings." suitable="Yes" type="QMED"/>
            <Usage notes="Above bankfull, flows are uncertain and gaugings do not include bypass flow." suitable="No" type="Pooling"/>
        </Usages>
    </GaugingStation>
